---
/* PostHog analytics snippet.

   `is:inline` prevents Astro from processing the script, which avoids TypeScript errors about
   `window.posthog` and keeps script ordering intact.

   TWO GUARDS, and both exist because of things that already went wrong on this site.

   1. HOSTNAME. Base.astro's GA4 block carries the note that every Vercel preview deployment
      (*.vercel.app) builds in production mode, so `import.meta.env.PROD` alone let preview and
      local-production traffic into the same property as real visitors and "quietly inflated it".
      PostHog shipped from the wizard with only the PROD check. It gets the same runtime hostname
      guard GA4 has, and the guard wraps the LOADER too — so off-domain builds skip the
      array.js request entirely rather than merely skipping init.

   2. TOKEN PRESENT. The wizard wrote `posthog.init(apiKey || '')`. An empty-string init is the
      worst available failure: PostHog constructs fine, every `capture()` call succeeds, nothing
      is ever recorded, and no error appears anywhere. Since `.env` is gitignored, a Vercel build
      missing PUBLIC_POSTHOG_PROJECT_TOKEN would have looked completely healthy while collecting
      nothing. Bail out loudly-by-absence instead: no key, no PostHog, and `window.posthog` stays
      undefined so every `window.posthog?.capture(...)` call site no-ops safely.
*/
import { site } from '../site.config.ts';
const liveHost = new URL(site.url).hostname;
---
<script
  is:inline
  define:vars={{
    apiKey: import.meta.env.PUBLIC_POSTHOG_PROJECT_TOKEN,
    apiHost: import.meta.env.PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST,
    liveHost,
  }}
>
  if (apiKey && (location.hostname === liveHost || location.hostname === 'www.' + liveHost)) {
